    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `StdlibChecker._check_open_call`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_check_open_call` function is a part of the `StdlibChecker` class in the `pylint/checkers/stdlib.py` file. It is designed to analyze calls to the `open` function or similar functions in the standard library, specifically focusing on the mode and encoding arguments. This function helps ensure that files are opened with appropriate modes and encodings, which is crucial for file handling operations.\n\nThe function takes three parameters: `node`, which is an instance of `nodes.Call` representing the function call being checked; `open_module`, a string indicating the module from which the function is being called (e.g., \"_io\" or \"pathlib\"); and `func_name`, a string representing the name of the function being called (e.g., \"open\" or \"read_text\").\n\nThe function does not return any value; instead, it adds messages to the checker's message queue if it detects issues with the mode or encoding arguments. It relies on several helper functions from the `utils` module, such as `get_argument_from_call`, `infer_kwarg_from_call`, and `safe_infer`, to extract and infer the values of the mode and encoding arguments.\n\nThe function handles errors by catching `utils.NoSuchArgumentError` exceptions when trying to retrieve arguments by position. If an argument is not found by position, it attempts to infer the argument by keyword. If the mode argument is not found or is invalid, it adds a \"bad-open-mode\" message. If the encoding argument is not specified or is `None`, it adds an \"unspecified-encoding\" message.\n\nThe function does not have any side effects other than adding messages to the checker's message queue. It does not modify any external state or data.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (